# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEFORE YOU START: Cross App Access (XAA/ID-JAG) — the mechanism the
# okta-upstream-token-exchange policy below relies on — is a young Okta
# feature (Agent SSO went GA 2026-08-24). A 30-day Workforce trial org
# usually unlocks this for evaluation, so it should be available — confirm
# under Security > API for a Cross App Access / Agent SSO section, or
# validate this project's idp/resourceAs shape first against Okta's hosted
# playground at https://xaa.dev (pre-wired, no tenant setup needed). If your
# org genuinely lacks it, "shared-oauth"/"user-oauth" authMode in
# policies.json still gets a working gateway, but is plain OAuth brokering,
# not a literal token exchange — see the README for that tradeoff.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Okta org — bare hostname, no scheme, no trailing slash, no path.
# e.g. dev-12345678.okta.com (Integrator Free Plan orgs look like this).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OKTA_DOMAIN=your-tenant.okta.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leg 1 — inbound browser login (mcp-okta-oauth / McpOktaOAuthInboundPolicy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Okta Admin Console > Applications > Create App Integration
#   Sign-in method: OIDC - OpenID Connect
#   Application type: Web Application
#   Grant type: Authorization Code (leave the rest at their defaults)
#   Sign-in redirect URI:
#     Local dev:   http://localhost:9000/__zuplo/oauth/callback
#     Deployed:    https://<gateway-host>/__zuplo/oauth/callback
#   Assign this app to whichever Okta group/users should be able to reach the
#   gateway at all (this is the *authentication* gate; role/tool gating is
#   handled separately by the tool-rbac policy below).
OKTA_GATEWAY_CLIENT_ID=
OKTA_GATEWAY_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tool RBAC (mcp-capability-filter-inbound)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# No env vars — config/policies.json gates the `echo-get` tool behind an
# Okta app role/group claim named `mcp-user` (see policies.json > tool-rbac).
# In Okta: Security > API > Authorization Servers > [server] > Claims, add a
# `roles` (or `groups`) claim to the access token sourced from the user's
# Okta group memberships, then add whoever should see/call `echo-get` to a
# group named `mcp-user` (or edit the role name in policies.json to match
# whatever claim/value your org already uses).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leg 2 — ID-JAG issuance (okta-upstream-token-exchange > idJag.idp)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This is the RFC 8693 token-exchange leg: the gateway presents the caller's
# Okta identity assertion and asks the SAME "default" custom authorization
# server used for browser login (authorizationServerId: "default" on
# okta-inbound-oauth, above) to mint an ID-JAG
# (requested_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:id-jag). Every Okta
# org ships with a custom AS literally named "default" — reusing it here
# (rather than the mostly-unconfigurable org authorization server at
# /oauth2/v1/...) is what makes claims/scopes/policies for this leg
# API-manageable at all.
#
# Token endpoint:
#   https://${OKTA_DOMAIN}/oauth2/default/v1/token
OKTA_IDJAG_TOKEN_URL=https://your-tenant.okta.com/oauth2/default/v1/token

# Okta Admin Console > Applications > Create App Integration
#   Sign-in method: API Services (a confidential/service app — no end-user
#   browser flow on this leg)
#   Client authentication: Public key / Private key (NOT a client secret —
#   Cross App Access expects a signed private_key_jwt client assertion, not
#   client_secret_post/basic). Generate or upload a key pair on the app's
#   General tab and paste the private key below; upload the public key/JWKS
#   to the app in Okta.
#   Then: Security > API > Authorization Servers > default > Access
#   Policies — add/edit a rule that allows this app's client_id the
#   `urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange` grant type. (This
#   specific step — granting a new OAuth grant type on the org-wide
#   "default" server — is the kind of change worth doing by hand or with
#   explicit sign-off rather than scripting blind, even though the rest of
#   this checklist is safe to automate via the Okta Management API.)
OKTA_IDJAG_CLIENT_ID=
OKTA_IDJAG_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leg 3 — ID-JAG redemption (okta-upstream-token-exchange > idJag.resourceAs)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Resource Authorization Server representing the downstream basic-api
# MCP server. Create a dedicated custom authorization server for it (Security
# > API > Authorization Servers > Add Authorization Server) rather than
# reusing "default" — its Audience field is what OKTA_RESOURCE_AS_AUDIENCE
# must match exactly (including a trailing slash if Okta added one).
#
# Token endpoint:
#   https://${OKTA_DOMAIN}/oauth2/${authServerId}/v1/token
OKTA_RESOURCE_AS_TOKEN_URL=https://your-tenant.okta.com/oauth2/YOUR_AUTH_SERVER_ID/v1/token

# The Audience value configured on that authorization server (Security > API
# > Authorization Servers > [server] > Settings > Audience) — NOT the
# basic-api URL itself, the Okta AS's own identifier.
OKTA_RESOURCE_AS_AUDIENCE=

# A second API Services app (or the same OKTA_IDJAG_CLIENT_ID app, if you
# gave it an access policy on this authorization server too) authorized on
# this Resource AS for the `urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer`
# grant type — this is what actually redeems the ID-JAG for the access token
# that gets attached to the call to basic-api. Same private_key_jwt
# requirement as the ID-JAG issuer app above.
OKTA_RESOURCE_CLIENT_ID=
OKTA_RESOURCE_PRIVATE_KEY_PEM=

# Add a custom scope on this authorization server named `basic-api.read`
# (Security > API > Authorization Servers > [server] > Scopes) to match
# config/policies.json's idJag.scopes, or edit that file to match whatever
# scope name you create.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Downstream MCP server this gateway fronts.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPSTREAM_BASIC_API_MCP_URL=https://basic-api-main-cf106ad.zuplo.app/mcp
